window.docContents[38].resolve({"/The-value-at-s-___-1/#An-introduction-to-p-adic-L-functions-___-Lean-blueprint--The-value-at-s-___-1":{"contents":"Throughout,  is an odd prime and  is a non-trivial Dirichlet\ncharacter, written in the usual form , where  has\nconductor  and  has conductor  prime to . We write\n for the conductor of , and let  denote a\nfixed primitive -th root of unity. We write  for the Gauss sum\nattached to .\n\nThe interpolation theorem identifies, for every integer , the integral\n with the classical -value\n. These  lie in the range of interpolation, and there the\nlink to classical values is explicit; such values are called critical. It is\nnatural to ask what happens outside this range — in particular at ,\ni.e. at . A priori such non-critical values need have nothing to do with\nclassical -values: indeed  is transcendental, so it cannot be\nviewed as a -adic number in any natural way. Nevertheless there is a formula\nfor the -adic -function at  strikingly parallel to its classical\nanalogue. The -adic formula is due to Leopoldt; it can be used to prove a\n-adic analogue of the analytic class number formula, and is the simplest\ninstance of the -adic Beilinson / Perrin-Riou conjectures, which describe\nnon-critical special values of -adic -functions in arithmetic terms.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n","context":"An introduction to p-adic L-functions — Lean blueprint","header":"6. The value at s = 1","id":"/The-value-at-s-___-1/#An-introduction-to-p-adic-L-functions-___-Lean-blueprint--The-value-at-s-___-1"}});